      "text": "To determine the number of different positive four-digit integers that can be formed using the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9, we need to consider the unique permutations of these digits. Since the digits 2 and 9 are repeated, we use the formula for permutations of a multiset:\n\n\\[ \\text{Number of permutations} = \\frac{n!}{n_1! \\cdot n_2! \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot n_k!} \\]\n\nwhere \\( n \\) is the total number of items to arrange, and \\( n_1, n_2, \\ldots, n_k \\) are the frequencies of the distinct items. Here, \\( n = 4 \\) (the total number of digits), and the digits 2 and 9 each appear twice.\n\nSo, the number of different four-digit integers is:\n\n\\[ \\text{Number of permutations} = \\frac{4!}{2! \\cdot 2!} \\]\n\nLet's calculate this using Python.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Total number of digits\nn = 4\n# Frequency of each digit (2 and 9)\nfreq_2 = 2\nfreq_9 = 2\n\n# Calculate the number of permutations\nnum_permutations = math.factorial(n) // (math.factorial(freq_2) * math.factorial(freq_9))\nprint(num_permutations)\n```\n```output\n6\n```\nThe number of different positive four-digit integers that can be formed using the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9 is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
